  <static seed="106" source="csafranski/2.4HW_prob6.pg">
    <statement><p>If <me>f(x) =  \frac { 4 x^2 \tan x }{ \sec x } ,</me> find <m>f'( x )</m>.</p>
<p><fillin characters="50" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>Find <m>f'( 5 )</m>.</p>
<p><fillin characters="50" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4\mathopen{}\left(2x\sin\mathopen{}\left(x\right)+x^{2}\cos\mathopen{}\left(x\right)\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-9.99075244020291</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>SOLUTION</p>
<p>We first simplify the function 
<me>f(x) = \frac{4 x^2 \tan x }{ \sec x } = 4 x^2  \frac{\sin x}{\cos x}\cos x 
= 4 x^2 \sin x</me>
The product rule then gives
<me>f'(x) = 4(  x^2 \cos x + 2x \sin x )</me>
Evaluating at <m>x = 5</m>:
<me>f'(5) = 100 \cos(5) + 40 \sin(5)</me></p>
</solution>
